@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Creating & Configuring Build Pipelines";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Build pipelines in Azure DevOps automate the process of compiling, testing, and packaging software, ensuring smooth and efficient software delivery.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is a Build Pipeline?</h2>
        <p>A **Build Pipeline** is an automated process that takes source code, compiles it, runs tests, and packages the application for deployment. It ensures consistent, repeatable, and reliable software builds.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Ensures **code quality** by running automated tests.</li>
            <li>Builds artifacts that can be deployed in multiple environments.</li>
            <li>Reduces manual work by automating repetitive tasks.</li>
            <li>Enables faster software delivery through automation.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/build-pipeline.png" alt="Azure Build Pipeline" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Azure DevOps Build Pipeline Workflow</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Build Pipeline Workflow</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Developers push code to **Azure Repos** (or GitHub, Bitbucket).</li>
                <li>The build pipeline triggers an **automated build process**.</li>
                <li>Code is compiled and **unit tests** are executed.</li>
                <li>Build artifacts are created and stored in Azure Artifacts.</li>
                <li>Build status is reported, and errors are flagged for review.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Steps to Create a Build Pipeline in Azure DevOps</h2>
        <p>Follow these steps to set up a build pipeline:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Navigate to Azure DevOps:</strong> Open your project in Azure DevOps.</li>
            <li><strong>Go to Pipelines:</strong> Click on "Pipelines" in the left menu.</li>
            <li><strong>Create a New Pipeline:</strong> Click "New Pipeline" and select your source repository.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose a Build Template:</strong> Select a predefined build template or configure your own.</li>
            <li><strong>Define Build Steps:</strong> Add tasks such as **restore dependencies, compile code, and run tests**.</li>
            <li><strong>Save and Run:</strong> Save your pipeline and trigger a manual run.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Build Pipeline Configuration</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Use YAML pipelines for **better version control** and flexibility.</li>
                <li>Set up **triggers** to run builds automatically on code changes.</li>
                <li>Integrate **unit and integration tests** to ensure code stability.</li>
                <li>Enable **build caching** to speed up compilation times.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/yaml-build-pipeline.png" alt="YAML Pipeline" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>YAML-based Azure Build Pipeline</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Best Practices for Build Pipelines</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Use **automated tests** to catch issues early.</li>
            <li>Optimize build steps to **reduce execution time**.</li>
            <li>Enable **artifact retention policies** to manage storage.</li>
            <li>Monitor pipeline performance using **Azure DevOps analytics**.</li>
            <li>Implement **code quality checks** before merging changes.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Creating and configuring build pipelines in Azure DevOps helps teams automate software builds, reduce errors, and improve software quality. By leveraging **CI/CD pipelines**, teams can streamline software delivery and accelerate release cycles.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **YAML vs Classic Pipelines in Azure DevOps**.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("WhatIsCICD", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: What is CI/CD?</a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("YAMLvsClassicPipelines", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">Next: YAML vs Classic Pipelines &rarr;</a>
    </div>
</div>